[Event "Strategy: the outpost, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/OmJykCAg"]
[FEN "2qr1r1k/p3b1pp/1p1p1p2/1Pp2P2/P1P1P1PP/5Q2/8/2N1R1RK w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2qr1r1k/p3b1pp/1p1p1p2/1Pp2P2/P1P1P1PP/5Q2/8/2N1R1RK_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ Today we think like a general. Plans, not tricks. First plan: the outpost — a square no enemy pawn can ever attack, where a piece can sit for good. How do we walk the knight to e6? [%csl Ge6][%cal Yc1d3,Gd3f4,Gf4e6] }
1. Nd3 { The knight sets off for e6 by way of f4 — two hops, and once it lands there no black pawn can ever chase it away. [%csl Ge6][%cal Gd3f4,Gf4e6] } (1. Ne2 { Also good: the same plan down a different road, e2 then f4 then e6. We are walking the d3 road today. [%cal Ge2f4] } 1... Rde8 2. Nf4 { The knight reaches f4 all the same, one hop from home on e6. [%csl Gf4] }) (1. g5 { A real pawn storm, and it wins too — but it makes him spend a move first, while f4 was free for the knight right now. } 1... g6 2. Nd3 { Even after the pawn storm, the knight still heads for f4. The outpost plan never went away. [%cal Gd3f4] }) (1. h5 { The h pawn runs on ahead with no piece behind it, and he simply stops it in its tracks. } 1... h6 { Your pawn on h5 is stuck for good, and the knight still has not taken a single step. [%csl Rh5] }) (1. e5 { A pawn shove with nothing following it up. } 1... dxe5 { He simply takes the free pawn. A clean pawn down, and the outpost plan never got started. [%csl Re5] }) 1... Rde8 { He doubles his rooks on the e column. Keep walking: the knight heads for f4 next. [%cal Yd3f4] } 2. Nf4 { From f4 the knight is one hop from e6, and no black pawn can ever reach either square to chase it off. [%csl Gf4,Ge6][%cal Gf4e6] } (2. g5 { Also good — the knight reaches f4 next move regardless of the pawn storm. } 2... fxg5 3. hxg5 Bd8 4. Nf4 { There it is: the knight reaches f4 all the same, just one move later. [%csl Gf4] }) (2. Rd1 { Also good: the open e column is tempting. But the knight's trip comes first, and the rook can follow after. } 2... g5 3. fxg6 hxg6 4. Nf4 { Now the knight joins in too, right on schedule. [%csl Gf4] }) (2. Kg2 { Tidy, and nothing is lost by it — but it is only a waiting move, and the knight still has the whole trip ahead of it. } 2... Qd7 3. Nf4 { The knight sets off for f4 regardless. The waiting move only cost a tempo. [%csl Gf4] }) 2... Bd8 { He shuffles his bishop out of the way. Now for the last jump: knight to e6. [%cal Yf4e6] } 3. Ne6 { Home! The knight sits on e6 for good. A rook or queen could reach it, but your f pawn guards the square, so any trade there only costs him material. [%csl Ge6] } (3. Rd1 { Also good: grab the open column first. But the plan was one hop from paying off — finish the knight's trip. } 3... Qd7 4. Rd5 Kg8 { Still winning, and e6 is still sitting empty, waiting for the knight whenever you want it. [%csl Re6] }) (3. g5 { Also good, another pawn storm. The knight settles for d5 instead of e6. } 3... fxg5 4. hxg5 Qa8 5. Nd5 { Another outpost, this time on d5 — also winning. [%csl Gd5] }) (3. Rg2 { A rook move. Fine, but the knight's trip was one hop from finished. } 3... Qd7 4. Rd2 Bc7 { Still winning. The outpost on e6 is still sitting there empty. [%csl Re6] }) (3. Kg2 { A waiting move. Also fine, but it spends a tempo the knight needed. } 3... Qd7 4. Rd1 Bc7 { Still winning, though the knight has still not made its final hop. }) (3. Qd3 { The queen eyes d6 instead. Also fine, but it is a different plan from the one we are learning. } 3... Qd7 4. h5 h6 { Still winning: his bishop on d8 has one square to go and nowhere after that, while your knight can still walk into e6 or d5 whenever you like. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the outpost, Fischer's knight"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/LGsz0l4h"]
[FEN "1r2kb1r/1bq3pp/p4pn1/2p1p3/NpP1P3/1P1QBN2/1P3PPP/2RR2K1 w k - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1r2kb1r/1bq3pp/p4pn1/2p1p3/NpP1P3/1P1QBN2/1P3PPP/2RR2K1_w_k_-_0_1"]

{ Same idea again: an outpost, guarded forever by your own pawns. Find a square deep in his camp where a knight can never be chased away, and send one there. }
1. Nb6 { He cannot recapture with the queen: taking on b6 opens the door to mate on d7. The knight stays put, one hop from home on d5. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gb6d5,Rd3d7] } (1. Ne1 { Also on its way to d5, by way of d3 — but it is the slower road. } 1... Be7 2. Qe2 O-O 3. Nd3 { You get there in the end, but he has had time to castle and tidy up while you took the long way. [%csl Rd3] }) (1. h4 { You poke his knight on g6, but the knight that needed moving was your own. } 1... Be7 2. h5 Rd8 { He develops and grabs the open column. Your own knight is still stuck on the rim, going nowhere. [%csl Ra4] }) (1. Nxc5 { Grabbing the pawn looks tempting, but it costs a whole piece. } 1... Bxc5 2. Bxc5 Qxc5 { The bishops trade off even, and what is left is a knight lost for one pawn. [%csl Rc5] }) (1. Qc2 { A quiet move, and the whole plan evaporates with it. } 1... Bd6 2. Rd2 Be7 { Nothing has happened. The knight on a4 is exactly where it was, still doing nothing. [%csl Ra4] }) 1... Rd8 { He challenges the open d column. Do not stop now — the knight still has one hop left. } 2. Nd5 { Home. The knight sits on d5 for good, and every black piece now has to live around it. [%csl Gd5] } (2. Qxd8+ { Also good: trade the rooks off first, and the knight lands on d5 anyway right after. } 2... Qxd8 3. Rxd8+ Kxd8 4. Nd5 { The same knight, the same square — you just got there with fewer pieces on the board. [%csl Gd5] }) (2. Qc2 { Careful: this leaves the knight on b6 hanging. } 2... Rxd1+ 3. Rxd1 Qxb6 { A whole knight is gone. Your queen sat on c2, three squares away from the piece it needed to guard. [%csl Rb6] }) (2. Qe2 { The queen steps aside and forgets all about the knight. } 2... Rxd1+ 3. Rxd1 Qxb6 { A whole knight is gone. Your queen went to e2 and simply lost sight of b6. [%csl Rb6] }) (2. Qd7+ { A check that costs you the queen for nothing in return. } 2... Rxd7 3. Nxd7 { A queen given away for a rook. That is a lost game. [%csl Rd7] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the outpost, a home for the knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/8QEYZaHM"]
[FEN "r2qkb1r/1b3ppp/p1n1p3/2ppPn2/1p3P2/2PP1NP1/PP2N1BP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 3 11"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qkb1r/1b3ppp/p1n1p3/2ppPn2/1p3P2/2PP1NP1/PP2N1BP/R1BQK2R_w_KQkq_-_3_11"]

{ Your knight on f5 is a monster — an outpost. He wants g4 to kick it away. How do you stop that for good? }
11. O-O { He castles, and g4 is coming next. How do you keep the knight on f5? [%csl Rg4] } 11... h5 { The pawn watches g4: if he ever pushes it, you just capture, and the knight on f5 never has to move. [%csl Gf5][%cal Gh5g4][%tail] } (11... bxc3 { You open the b column, but for whom? } 12. bxc3 h5 13. Rb1 { His rook is on b1 first. Playable, but you helped him get there. [%csl Rb1] }) (11... c4 { Too early: now g4 comes with an extra hit on your pawn chain. } 12. g4 cxd3 13. Qxd3 { The knight has to move now, and his pawns start rolling forward. [%cal Rg4f5] }) (11... g6 { You guard f5 from the wrong side — this pawn never touches that square. [%csl Rf5] } 12. g4 Ng7 { Kicked back to g7 — the saddest square a knight can stand on. [%csl Rg7] }) (11... Be7 { Development is nice, but the knight had to move first. } 12. g4 Nh4 13. Nxh4 Bxh4 { The knight is traded off, and the outpost is gone for good. [%csl Rf5] }) (11... Nh4 { Straight at his king, but straight into two different captures too. } 12. Nxh4 { Gone for nothing — even the pawn on g3 could have taken it. [%cal Rg3h4] }) 12. a3 12... a5 13. h3 13... Be7 { The bishop steps to e7 and you are ready to castle. His pawns on f4, g3 and h3 still cannot budge the knight on f5 — push g4 and you simply capture it back. [%csl Gf5] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, keep the outpost"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/4Ts0CCtQ"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/3b1ppp/3p4/2nPp3/1p2N3/6P1/1P2PPBP/1QR2RK1 b - - 0 22"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2q1rk1/3b1ppp/3p4/2nPp3/1p2N3/6P1/1P2PPBP/1QR2RK1_b_-_-_0_22"]

{ The right trade: which piece do you send off to keep your knight on c5 forever? [%csl Gc5] }
22... Bf5 { Bishop to f5: it offers itself for the knight on e4, the one piece that can ever chase yours off c5. His own bishop on g2 stares at a wall of pawns. [%cal Gf5e4][%csl Rg2] } (22... Nb3 { Also strong — a sharp trick that wins the exchange outright. But today's lesson is the trade, not the tactic. } 23. Nxd6 Qb8 24. Ne4 Nxc1 25. Qxc1 { A rook for a knight and a pawn — good for you, just not the plan we are here to learn. }) (22... Nxe4 { You traded the wrong knight. Yours on c5 was the hero of the position. } 23. Qxe4 Qb6 24. Rc4 { His queen sits happily in the middle now, and your b pawn is a target. Equal. [%csl Rb4] }) (22... Nb7 { The knight retreats from the best square on the board. } 23. Rc4 f5 24. Rxb4 { The b pawn is simply gone. [%csl Rb4] }) (22... Qb6 { You guard the b pawn, but miss the real point — it was the knight on c5 that needed keeping. } 23. Nxc5 dxc5 24. Rfd1 { He trades the knight off without a fight. His bishop lives, and your pawns are a mess. [%csl Rc5,Rb4] }) 23. Rc4 { He swings his rook over to c4, hitting the b pawn. [%csl Rb4] } 23... b3 { Safe on b3, and it pins his b2 pawn in place for good — that pawn is the new target. [%csl Rb2] } (23... Rb8 { You guard the pawn from behind, but it is still stuck on b4 and still a target. } 24. b3 Rb5 25. Qa1 { The pawn is stuck and his rook still sits on c4. Only a little better for you. [%csl Rb4] }) (23... Bxe4 { The right trade, just one move early. } 24. Bxe4 g6 25. Rxb4 { The b pawn went for nothing, and the trade you wanted has already happened anyway. [%csl Rb4] }) (23... g6 { A useful move, but the b pawn is left hanging. } 24. Rxb4 Bxe4 25. Bxe4 { A pawn is gone. Equal: your knight on c5 is worth at least as much as his extra pawn on d5. }) 24. Qc1 { He steps his queen off the pin, freeing the knight to move. } 24... Bxe4 { Now the trade. His good piece is gone, and the knight on c5 stays for good. [%csl Gc5] } (24... Na6 { The knight wanders off c5, and the whole plan goes with it. } 25. Nd2 Nc5 26. Qc3 { Back where it started — his knight has escaped. Equal. [%csl Rd2] }) (24... Nxe4 { The wrong knight trades again. } 25. Bxe4 Bxe4 26. Rxe4 { Everything is traded off and his rook is the active piece now. Equal. }) (24... Na4 { The knight goes hunting on the rim instead. } 25. Nd2 Nc5 26. Qc3 { And it simply walks back. Nothing gained. }) 25. Bxe4 { He recaptures on e4 with the bishop. [%csl Re4] } 25... f5 { The pawn shoos the bishop away. Knight against bad bishop, and the b2 pawn is a target for good. [%csl Gc5,Rb2][%cal Gf5e4] } (25... Ra4 { Also good: trade a pair of rooks and keep squeezing. } 26. Rxa4 Nxa4 27. Kg2 { Still clearly better for you. [%csl Ga4] }) (25... Qd7 { Fine too, but why not chase the bishop first? } 26. Bb1 Qb5 { Still better for you, but the bishop got away. }) (25... g6 { Also fine — the bishop can still sit on e4 for now. } 26. Bd3 Qf6 { Still better for you, just a little slower. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, the stonewall bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/lPzDl6I7"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/pp4pp/2pbpn2/3p1p2/2PP4/1P3NP1/P3PPBP/RNBQ1RK1 b kq - 0 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqk2r/pp4pp/2pbpn2/3p1p2/2PP4/1P3NP1/P3PPBP/RNBQ1RK1_b_kq_-_0_7"]

{ Another right trade: his dark bishop on d6 guards every hole in his camp. Which piece should you send in to challenge it? [%csl Rd6,Ge5,Gc5] }
7... O-O { He castles. One piece holds his whole position together — which is it? [%csl Rd6] } 8. Ba3 { Bishop to a3: offer the trade for his dark-square defender. Once it is gone, e5, c5 and f4 all become yours. [%cal Ga3d6][%csl Ge5,Gc5] } (8. Nc3 { Development, but his bishop on d6 lives on and keeps guarding everything. } 8... Qe7 9. e3 Ne4 { He is comfortable, and the dark squares are still his. [%csl Rd6] }) (8. Bb2 { The bishop hides behind its own pawn and only stares at d4. } 8... Nbd7 9. Qc2 Ne4 { An ordinary game. The trade on d6 was the whole point, and this move never went for it. [%csl Rb2] }) (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but his bishop on d6 will simply chase it or trade it off. } 8... Nbd7 9. Nd3 Qe7 { Nothing is gained: your knight sits early on d3, but his bishop on d6 is still there, unchallenged. }) (8. e3 { Fine — the trade on a3 can still come next move. } 8... Bd7 9. Ba3 Bxa3 10. Nxa3 { The same trade, just one move later. Also good. [%csl Ga3] }) 8... Bxa3 { He takes. Recapture with the knight, and send it round toward d3. } 9. Nxa3 { The knight sets off round the houses: c2, then e1, then d3. [%cal Ga3c2,Gc2e1,Ge1d3] } 9... Qe7 { He eyes your knight. Where is it headed? } 10. Nc2 { Round to c2 first. One more hop to e1, then d3 — deep in your own camp. [%cal Gc2e1,Ge1d3][%csl Gd3][%tail] } (10. Qc1 { Also good: the queen takes over the a3 to f8 diagonal instead. } 10... Nbd7 11. Qb2 { The same idea, carried out with the queen this time. Fine. [%cal Gb2f6] }) (10. Nb1 { Back home first. It still reaches d2, but by the slow road. } 10... Nbd7 11. Qc1 e5 { He frees his own position while you are still regrouping. }) (10. c5 { The pawn goes forward and only becomes a target. } 10... b6 11. b4 a5 { He undermines it at once. Equal. [%csl Rc5] }) (10. b4 { A free pawn, handed straight to him. } 10... Qxb4 { Simply gone: his queen scoops up the pawn, and there is no way to win it back. [%csl Rb4] }) 10... Nbd7 11. Rc1 11... b6 12. Nce1 12... Ba6 13. Nd3 { Home on d3, shielded by your own c and d pawns — nothing black owns is anywhere near it. [%csl Gd3] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the right trade, keep his weaknesses"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/tPkvSFOq"]
[FEN "r2qkb1r/pp2p1pp/2p3b1/3np3/8/2P1B2P/PP1NBPP1/R2QK2R w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qkb1r/pp2p1pp/2p3b1/3np3/8/2P1B2P/PP1NBPP1/R2QK2R_w_KQkq_-_0_1"]

{ Another right trade: his e pawns are already doubled, and they only get worse as pieces come off. Which piece do you send in? }
1. Bh5 { Bishop to h5: offer the trade. His bishop on g6 is his best piece, and it is the one holding his h7 pawn together. [%cal Gh5g6] } (1. h4 { Useful later, but not yet — ignoring the centre lets him strike first. } 1... Nxe3 2. fxe3 Bf7 { He trades for the bishop pair, and now your king side pawns are wrecked too. Only a little better. [%csl Re3] }) (1. O-O { Safe, but slow — the trade you came for is still waiting. } 1... Bf5 2. Bg5 g6 { He tidies up while you castle. A little better for you, no more. }) (1. Bg5 { Not the trade you want — his bishop is after something else entirely. } 1... Qc7 2. Bh5 Bxh5 3. Qxh5+ g6 { The trade comes anyway, but his queen got there with an extra tempo. About equal. }) (1. Nc4 { The knight eyes e5, and he simply guards it. } 1... Qc7 2. Bh5 Bxh5 3. Qxh5+ g6 { Nothing gained: the same trade happens two moves later, and the knight on c4 was never needed for it. }) (1. Bf3 { It attacks the knight, but he has a counter-shot of his own. } 1... Nxe3 2. fxe3 e6 { He wins a bishop for a knight, and your pawns are wrecked instead of his. He stands better now. [%csl Re3] }) 1... e6 { He tucks the bishop away and keeps it. Trade for it anyway. } 2. Bxg6+ { Take it: his best piece is gone, and it was the one piece propping up his weak, doubled e pawns. [%csl Rg6] } (2. Bf3 { You back off instead, and your own bishop on e3 is left hanging. } 2... Nxe3 3. fxe3 Qh4+ { A wrecked centre and a king caught in the open. That is lost. [%csl Re3] }) (2. Ne4 { A jump into the middle. It works, but the trade you already had lined up was simpler. } 2... Qh4 3. Bxg6+ hxg6 { You get the trade anyway. Also fine. }) (2. O-O { Castle first, and he trades the bishop off himself. } 2... Qf6 3. Bxg6+ Qxg6 { His queen lands happily in the centre, delighted to recapture there. Only a little better. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/6x6tlAtk"]
[FEN "r3nrk1/1q3ppp/p1b1pb2/8/1PB2B2/P1N3Q1/5PPP/2RR2K1 b - - 0 21"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3nrk1/1q3ppp/p1b1pb2/8/1PB2B2/P1N3Q1/5PPP/2RR2K1_b_-_-_0_21"]

{ New plan: improve your worst piece. Your knight on e8 is doing nothing at all — give it a road to a real square. [%csl Re8][%cal Yg7g6,Ge8g7,Gg7f5] }
21... g6 { The pawn opens g7 for the knight. From there it can jump to f5, its dream square. [%cal Ge8g7,Gg7f5][%csl Gf5] } (21... Be7 { The bishop steps back, and the knight on e8 is still fast asleep. } 22. Bf1 Rc8 23. h3 Nf6 { It gets out by a slower road in the end. Still a little worse than improving the knight straight away. [%csl Re8] }) (21... Kh8 { A waiting move. The knight is still asleep on e8. } 22. Bf1 Be7 23. Be5 { He gets to improve his own worst piece too. Slightly worse for you. }) (21... Rd8 { You trade a pair of rooks, and the knight still has nowhere to go. } 22. Bf1 Rxd1 23. Rxd1 Be7 { Nothing is solved. A little worse — the knight on e8 is exactly as stuck as before. }) (21... Rc8 { The rook moves, and the knight keeps waiting. } 22. Bf1 Be7 23. Rd4 { He gets to improve first. A little better for him — improve your own worst piece before he improves his. }) 22. Bg5 { He fights for the dark squares and hits your bishop. Keep to the plan. [%cal Yf6g7] } 22... Bg7 { The bishop steps to g7, and the knight's road is clear: e8 to f6 is open. [%cal Ge8f6] } (22... Bh8 { Also good. The bishop tucks into the corner and g7 stays free for the knight anyway. } 23. Qf4 Bg7 24. Qh4 Nc7 { The same plan, carried out a move later. }) (22... Bxg5 { You give up the bishop pair, but the knight still gets out. Also fine. } 23. Qxg5 Ng7 24. Bd3 Rfd8 { The knight is headed for f5 next. [%cal Gg7f5] }) (22... Rc8 { A rook move while your bishop sits under attack. } 23. Bd3 Bxg5 24. Qxg5 Ng7 { Fine, but a little slower than dealing with the bishop first. }) (22... h5 { You weaken your own king side for nothing. } 23. Bd3 Rd8 24. Bxf6 Nxf6 { The bishop pair is gone and your king has drafty squares around it. Worse. [%csl Rh5] }) 23. Bf1 { He tucks his bishop away. Now the knight jumps. [%cal Ye8f6] } 23... Nf6 { The knight lives. From f6 it reaches d5 and e4, and it is your best piece on the board now. [%csl Gf6][%cal Gf6d5,Gf6e4] } (23... h6 { Also good: kick the bishop first, and the knight jumps right after. } 24. Bf4 Nf6 { It comes out anyway, just one move later. [%csl Gf6] }) (23... h5 { Fine too, but the knight was already ready to jump this move. } 24. h3 Nf6 { The same knight lands on the same square, just a move later. }) (23... Rc8 { The rook moves, and his bishop gets to h6 instead. } 24. Qh4 Nc7 25. Bh6 { He trades off your good bishop. A little worse for you. [%csl Rh6] }) (23... Bf6 { You offer the very trade you were avoiding. } 24. Bxf6 Nxf6 25. Qe5 { The bishop pair is gone and his queen sits happily in the middle. Worse for you. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, the sleeping rook"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/r6CnOaY7"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/pp4p1/7p/3p4/P1pPp3/2P1P3/1Q3PPP/R1B2RK1 w - - 0 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/pp4p1/7p/3p4/P1pPp3/2P1P3/1Q3PPP/R1B2RK1_w_-_-_0_18"]

{ Same idea again: find your worst piece a job. Your rook on a8 is still asleep — give it a road to the king side. }
18. Qb5 { His queen eyes b7 and d5. Which of your pieces most needs a job right now? [%csl Ra8] } 18... a5 { The a pawn steps aside, and the rook has a door: out to a6, then across to g6. [%cal Ga8a6,Ga6g6] } (18... a6 { Also good: the same door, just one square smaller. } 19. Qb1 a5 20. Ba3 Rf7 { The rook comes out next move either way. Still winning. [%csl Ga8] }) (18... Rf7 { The other rook lifts first, but the one on a8 is still asleep. } 19. a5 Bg4 20. Ba3 { His bishop gets out and a5 shuts your rook's door. Still better for you, but less. [%csl Ra5] }) (18... Bg4 { The bishop comes out, but where is the a rook supposed to go? } 19. Ba3 Rf7 20. Qc5 { His pieces wake up while yours is still stuck. Only a little better. }) (18... Rf6 { The f rook wanders sideways, and the a rook stays asleep. } 19. Ba3 Kh7 20. Qb1 { His bishop gets out and you are only a little better for it. [%csl Ra8] }) (18... Qg5 { The queen attacks all on her own, with nobody backing her up. } 19. Kh1 Qh5 20. Ba3 { Nobody comes to help her. Only a little better. }) 19. Ra2 { He tucks his rook away out of harm's reach. Now bring yours out. } 19... Ra6 { Out along the sixth row, off the back rank at last. [%cal Ga6g6][%csl Rg2][%tail] } (19... Rf7 { Also good. The f rook first, the a rook next. } 20. Ba3 Ra6 21. Qb1 Qg5 { Both rooks are coming into the game now. Winning. [%cal Ga6g6] }) (19... Rf5 { The rook goes up the f column instead. Fine, but the a rook still waits. } 20. f3 exf3 21. Rxf3 Rxf3 22. gxf3 { His king is open, but you have traded off your attacker. Still better. }) (19... Rf3 { Bold, and it gets stuck out there with nothing to do. } 20. Kh1 Rf7 21. f4 { You have to bring it back. Still better, but the rook lost precious time. [%csl Rf3] }) (19... Rf6 { Sideways, and it blocks the sixth row for the other rook. } 20. Qb1 Raa6 21. Ba3 { Now two rooks are standing in each other's way. Still better. }) 20. Ba3 20... Rf5 { The other rook lifts to f5. Both rooks are finally in the game, and his bishop still has nothing to bite on. [%csl Gf5,Ga6] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, do nothing well"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/uzv8EH7h"]
[FEN "r1q1r1k1/1p1n1pBp/p3p1p1/P1np4/5P2/3P1NP1/R1P1QPBP/1R4K1 b - - 0 22"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1q1r1k1/1p1n1pBp/p3p1p1/P1np4/5P2/3P1NP1/R1P1QPBP/1R4K1_b_-_-_0_22"]

{ Same idea, a different piece: your knight on f3 needs a job too. He is tangled up over there — regroup quietly, and do not let him escape it. }
22... Kxg7 { He takes his bishop back with the king. His pieces are still knotted together — what is your plan now? [%csl Rc5,Rd7] } 23. Nd2 { A quiet knight move: it eyes e4 and c4 and gives him nothing to grab at. He stays exactly as tangled as before. [%cal Gd2e4,Gd2c4] } (23. c4 { You rush the position open, and that is exactly what untangles him for free. } 23... dxc4 24. Ne5 Nxe5 25. Qxe5+ { Playable, but his knots have come undone. [%csl Gc5] }) (23. Rb4 { The rook climbs to the fourth row with nothing there to attack. } 23... Qc7 24. Qe1 Nf6 { It did nothing useful there. Still better for you, but you gained nothing extra either. }) (23. Ne5 { You trade off your good knight. } 23... Nxe5 24. Qxe5+ Kg8 { Trades are exactly what the tangled side wants. Still better for you, but less. }) (23. h4 { A pawn move on the wrong side of the board. } 23... Rb8 24. h5 Qc7 { He gets to unwind while you push pawns elsewhere. Still better for you, but less. }) 23... Nf6 { He brings his knight back home. Now push the pawn. } 24. d4 { The pawn hits his knight on c5, and it has nowhere good to land. [%cal Gd4c5][%csl Rc5] } (24. c4 { Also good, but this lets the knight stay on c5 a little longer. } 24... h5 25. d4 Nce4 { The same idea arrives a move later. Still better for you. }) (24. h4 { Slow: a king-side pawn push does nothing about the knight stuck on c5. } 24... Rd8 25. Qe5 h5 { He gets time to organise himself. Only a little better for you now. }) (24. Qe3 { The queen eyes c5, and he pushes his own pawn with tempo. } 24... Rb8 25. c4 d4 { His pawn hits your queen. Less for you than the plan deserved. }) 24... Nce4 { The knight jumps to e4. Trade it off. } 25. Bxe4 { Take with the bishop first. [%cal Gd2e4] } (25. Nxe4 { Also good — the same trade, just in the other order. } 25... dxe4 26. Bxe4 Nxe4 27. Qxe4 { A pawn up. [%csl Ge4] }) (25. Rba1 { You ignore the knight on e4, and it forks your rooks. } 25... Nc3 26. Qe5 Nxa2 { A whole rook is gone. [%csl Ra2] }) (25. c4 { A pawn move while a knight still sits on e4. } 25... Nc3 26. Qd3 Nxa2 { The same fork lands again. A whole rook is gone. }) 25... dxe4 { He recaptures. Now collect. } 26. Nxe4 { A pawn up, and his knot of pieces has turned into a lost pawn. [%csl Ge4] } (26. Rab2 { The rook steps away, and he finds a hit of his own. } 26... Qc3 27. Nxe4 Nxe4 28. Qxe4 Qxa5 { You won a pawn, he won the a pawn back. Only a little better for you. }) (26. Rb4 { Slow: the rook takes a detour while the pawn on e4 is still there for the taking. } 26... Qc6 27. c4 Red8 { He holds everything together. Only a little better for you. }) (26. Nc4 { The knight goes the wrong way. } 26... Nd5 27. Rb3 Qc7 { His knight settles safely on d5. Only a little better for you. }) (26. Qxe4 { The queen steps right onto a square his knight can reach. } 26... Nxe4 { Gone: his knight simply takes yours right back on e4. [%csl Re4] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: improve your worst piece, stop his plan"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/ckZZnXY6"]
[FEN "r1b2bk1/1pq3pp/p2pp3/4n1PB/3QP3/2N1B3/PPP4P/5RK1 w - - 0 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b2bk1/1pq3pp/p2pp3/4n1PB/3QP3/2N1B3/PPP4P/5RK1_w_-_-_0_18"]

{ He wants to play bishop to d7 and then c6, and he would be fine. Your knight on c3 is doing nothing at all. Fix both problems with one move. }
18. Nd5 { Knight to d5: if the pawn takes, your queen recaptures with check. And if he tries bishop to d7 instead, it walks straight into a fork on b6. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd5b6] } (18. Bf4 { A natural developing move, but it lets him play the very move you needed to stop. } 18... Bd7 19. Qf2 Be8 { His bishop gets out safely. Equal. [%csl Gd7] }) (18. Na4 { The knight heads to the rim, and a pawn simply kicks it away. } 18... b5 19. Nb6 Rb8 20. Nxc8 { Traded off for nothing extra. Equal. }) (18. h4 { A pawn move, and the bishop gets out just the same. } 18... Bd7 19. Qd2 Be8 { His plan worked: a rook pawn push bought him exactly the tempo he needed for bishop to d7. Slightly worse for you. [%csl Gd7] }) (18. Qd2 { The queen steps back, and the bishop comes out regardless. } 18... Bd7 19. Bd4 g6 { His plan worked all the same — the queen retreat let the bishop out just as well. Slightly worse for you. }) 18... Qc6 { He steps his queen aside and hits your knight. Where does it go? } 19. Nb6 { To b6. His rook has to move, and his bishop on c8 still has not budged an inch. [%csl Gb6,Rc8][%cal Gb6c8] } (19. Nb4 { Also playable, but the queen comes straight back and you end up dancing in circles. } 19... Qc7 20. Nd5 Qc6 { Back where you both started. }) (19. Qb6 { You offer the queen trade, and he is delighted to take it. } 19... Bd7 20. Qxc6 Bxc6 { His bishop gets out and the queens are gone. Slightly worse for you. [%csl Gc6] }) (19. Rxf8+ { You trade the rook and lose the thread of the whole plan. } 19... Kxf8 20. Nb6 Rb8 { The knight still lands on b6, but you gave up the f column to get there. Slightly worse for you. }) (19. Nc3 { Back home again, and his bishop comes straight out. } 19... Bd7 20. Be2 b5 { His plan worked. Worse for you. [%csl Gd7] }) 1-0


