[FEN "r4rk1/pbpp2pp/1p2q3/5n2/1nPP1B2/2NQ1PPB/PP5P/2KR3R w - - 1 15"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/pbpp2pp/1p2q3/5n2/1nPP1B2/2NQ1PPB/PP5P/2KR3R_w_-_-_1_15"]

{ His king sits on c1, and his light-squared bishop is stuck all the way over on h3. Which colour is weak around a king like that? }
15. Qf1 { His queen tucks in behind his own king on f1. Where do you send yours? } 15... Qg6 { Queen to g6: safely out of harm's way, and it already stares straight down the light diagonal at his king on b1. [%cal Gg6b1][%csl Gg6] } (15... Qf6 { It aims at two pawns, but it leaves your own knight on b4 hanging. } 16. a3 g5 17. Be5 { The knight is trapped with nowhere to run. That is a lost piece. [%csl Rb4] }) (15... g5 { You poke his bishop, and he pokes your knight right back. } 16. a3 gxf4 17. axb4 { A messy trade all round. Equal. }) (15... Ba6 { The bishop hits a rook, but nothing actually comes of it. } 16. b3 g5 17. a3 { Your knight is the one in trouble now. Worse for you. [%csl Rb4] }) (15... a5 { You guard the knight, but the queen was always the real point of this plan. } 16. a3 g5 17. d5 { He stands better now. }) (15... Bxf3 { A free pawn? His queen simply takes it back and hits your knight on the way. } 16. Qxf3 g5 17. a3 { A whole piece is lost. [%csl Rb4,Rf5] }) 16. Bxf5 { He trades off his bishop for your knight. Take back with the queen. } 16... Qxf5 { The queen sits on f5, on the light squares, staring straight at his king. [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf5b1] } (16... Rxf5 { The rook takes instead, and the queen loses her diagonal at his king. } 17. Rd2 Qf7 18. d5 { His centre rolls forward and your queen is off the diagonal for good. Worse for you. [%csl Rf5] }) 17. Rd2 { He covers c2. Now go after the bishop. [%csl Gc2] } 17... g5 { The pawn kicks the bishop, and the f3 pawn falls next to your bishop on b7. [%cal Gg5f4,Gb7f3][%csl Rf3] } (17... d5 { A solid move, but his a pawn kicks your knight in return. } 18. a3 a5 19. axb4 axb4 { A mess. Equal. }) (17... a5 { You guard the knight, and he breaks open the centre instead. } 18. d5 c6 19. dxc6 Bxc6 { Equal: the centre opened up, and the trade left nothing for either side to grab. }) (17... Rae8 { The rook waits, and your knight gets kicked all the same. } 18. d5 d6 19. a3 { The knight has to go home. Slightly worse for you. [%csl Rb4] }) (17... d6 { Slow, and his pawns start to roll. } 18. h4 a5 19. a3 { Worse for you: a3 kicks the knight, and you have not started anything on the king side yet. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the weak colour, the light squares"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/Ye4KmNfP"]
[FEN "2r3k1/1p1qbppp/p1r1pn2/2Pp4/PP1B4/4P3/4BPPP/2RQ1RK1 b - - 2 20"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/1p1qbppp/p1r1pn2/2Pp4/PP1B4/4P3/4BPPP/2RQ1RK1_b_-_-_2_20"]

{ Another weak colour: he has already lost his light-squared bishop, so the light squares are yours for good. But first a rook is blocking your pawns. }
20... R6c7 { The rook steps back to a passive square. Which of your pieces can gain a move by attacking it? [%csl Rc7] } 21. Be5 { Bishop to e5, hitting the rook. A move gained, and it clears d4 for your queen too. [%cal Ge5c7][%csl Gd4] } (21. Qb3 { The queen guards b5, but he plays a5 himself and the whole position freezes over. [%csl Gb5] } 21... a5 22. Be5 axb4 23. Bxc7 Qxc7 { Still better for you, but the pawns are locked in place. [%csl Rb4] }) (21. a5 { Also good, but this way the rook is never chased off its square. } 21... Ne8 22. Be5 Bd6 { A little slower. Still better for you. }) (21. Qc2 { A quiet move, and he gets his own a5 push in first. } 21... a5 22. Bb5 Qd8 { Your pawns are frozen. Equal. [%csl Ra5] }) (21. b5 { Too soon: the pawn break comes before the bishop even reaches e5. } 21... axb5 22. Be5 Rxc5 { The c pawn is gone. Worse for you. [%csl Rc5] }) (21. Bxf6 { You trade your good bishop for his knight. } 21... Bxf6 22. a5 g6 { His remaining bishop is happy now. Only a little better for you. [%csl Gf6] }) (21. Rb1 { Slow, and he gets his a5 push in anyway. } 21... a5 22. Be5 axb4 { Only a little better for you: the rook move gained nothing, and now his a pawn is gone too. }) 21... Rc6 { The rook retreats to c6. Now for the light squares. } 22. a5 { Pawn to a5: b6 belongs to you now, his backward b pawn has nowhere safe to go, and his own bishop has no light squares left to bite on. [%csl Gb6,Rb7][%cal Gb4b5] } (22. h3 { Fine, but a5 was already sitting ready to play. } 22... Ne4 23. Bd3 Bf6 { Still better for you: a5 could have gone straight in, and h3 only delayed it. }) (22. Qd4 { The queen comes to d4 first. Also fine. } 22... Qd8 23. a5 Qf8 { Still better for you: the same plan arrives just as well, one tempo later. }) (22. Bd3 { The bishop moves first. Also fine. } 22... Qd8 23. a5 g6 { Still better for you: a5 still lands, and his king-side pawn move did not help him at all. }) (22. b5 { The pawn break, but a move too soon. } 22... Rxc5 23. Rxc5 Rxc5 24. bxa6 bxa6 { The c pawn is gone. Only slightly better for you. [%csl Rc5] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the weak colour, remove the guard"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/kVfjx5NJ"]
[FEN "r3n1k1/p1q2pb1/bpp1p1p1/3p2QN/5P2/1P2P3/1BPP2PP/1K5R w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3n1k1/p1q2pb1/bpp1p1p1/3p2QN/5P2/1P2P3/1BPP2PP/1K5R_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ All his pawns are stuck on light squares. Something of his is still holding every dark square together — clear it off the board. }
1. Nxg7 { The knight takes, not the bishop — your own dark-squared bishop is the attacker here and has to stay on the board. Queen to f6 comes next. [%csl Gb2][%cal Gg5f6][%tail] } (1. Bxg7 { The wrong piece takes: this is the bishop you need to keep. } 1... Nxg7 2. Nf6+ Kf8 { He holds, and your attack is over — your dark-squared bishop was the real attacker all along. Close to equal. [%csl Rf6] }) (1. Qh6 { The queen jumps in first, and his bishop simply takes it. } 1... Bxh6 { A whole queen, gone in one move. [%csl Rh6] }) (1. Be5 { Slow: it gives him time to get his queen to safety before you even touch g7. } 1... Qd8 2. Nxg7 Qxg5 3. fxg5 Nxg7 { The queens come off and he ends up a pawn ahead. Worse for you. }) (1. Nf6+ { The knight gives itself away for nothing. } 1... Nxf6 2. Bxf6 Bxf6 3. Qxf6 Re8 { Everything gets traded off, and your queen is left alone. That is lost. }) 1... Nxg7 { He recaptures with the knight — the natural try, but f8 with the king would have fought on much longer. [%csl Rg7] } 2. Qf6 2... Kf8 3. Qxg7+ { Check! The queen takes the knight for free — your bishop on b2 guards the diagonal, so he cannot take back. [%csl Ge7] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: prophylaxis, lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/t8L5VOxb"]
[FEN "2r3k1/pq3pp1/5b1p/8/2pr1N2/P5P1/1PQ1RP1P/5RK1 b - - 0 27"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/pq3pp1/5b1p/8/2pr1N2/P5P1/1PQ1RP1P/5RK1_b_-_-_0_27"]

{ New plan: prophylaxis — ask what he wants, then stop it before he gets it. He dreams of the back row, and of pushing h5 and f5. [%csl Re8,Rh5,Rf5][%cal Yg7g6] }
27... g6 { One pawn move does it all: g7 is free for your king, and both h5 and f5 are taken away from his knight and queen. [%csl Gg7,Gh5,Gf5] } (27... Rcd8 { You double your rooks, but the back row is still just as thin. } 28. Rfe1 Qc6 29. Nh5 { His knight lands on h5 and hits your bishop. Still a little better for you, but he still has play. [%csl Rh5] }) (27... Rdd8 { Back again, and the back row is still just as much of a worry. } 28. h4 Qf3 29. Re3 { Only a little better for you: the rook retreat left the back row exactly as thin as it was. }) (27... Qf3 { The queen jumps in on her own, with nobody backing her up. } 28. Re3 Qb7 29. Ne2 { She has to go back home. Only a little better for you. }) (27... h5 { You stop h5 with a pawn, and his knight simply crashes through it. } 28. Nxh5 Bg5 { A pawn is gone. Worse for you. [%csl Rh5] }) (27... Rxf4 { Trading a rook for a knight, for no good reason. } 28. gxf4 Qf3 29. Qf5 { A rook given for a knight, and nothing to show for it. Worse for you. }) 28. h4 { He grabs space: h5 is his idea now. Ask what he wants next, and cover that too. [%cal Yb7d7] } 28... Qd7 { Queen to d7: it eyes g4 and h3, and it guards the whole back row at once. [%cal Gd7g4][%csl Gd7] } (28... h5 { You stop h5 with a pawn, and his knight crashes straight through it. } 29. Nxg6 fxg6 30. Qxg6+ Qg7 { Three pawns for the knight, with more checks on the way. Most of your edge is gone. [%csl Rg6] }) (28... Kf8 { The king goes to the wrong side. } 29. h5 Qd7 30. Rc1 { Only a little better for you: the king walked the wrong way, and h5 arrived anyway. }) (28... Rdd8 { The rook goes back, giving up the d column in the process. } 29. Rfe1 c3 30. bxc3 { Only a little better for you: giving up the d column costs more than the pawn you won back. }) (28... Rd6 { Passive: the rook steps back and does nothing about his back-row ideas. } 29. Rfe1 c3 30. Re8+ Rxe8 31. Rxe8+ Kg7 { About equal. The back row problem is right back again! [%csl Re8] }) 29. Rfe1 { He doubles his rooks. Now think about the king. [%cal Yg8g7] } 29... Kg7 { The king steps up: no more back-row tricks for him to dream about. Now h5 is your idea, not his. [%csl Gg7][%cal Gh6h5] } (29... Kf8 { The wrong square — his knight crashes in on g6. } 30. Nxg6+ fxg6 31. Qxg6 Qf7 32. Qxh6+ { Three pawns for the knight, and your king stands bare. Only a little better for you. [%csl Rg6] }) (29... Qg4 { The queen wanders off on her own. } 30. Re4 Qd7 { She has to come straight back. Only a little better for you. }) (29... Rf8 { A rook move while the king was the whole point. } 30. h5 g5 31. Ng2 { Equal: the rook move ignored the king, and his king-side play came anyway. }) (29... Rxf4 { A rook for a knight again, with nothing gained. } 30. gxf4 Qg4+ 31. Kf1 Qh3+ { A draw by checks. Nothing more. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: prophylaxis, what does he threaten"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/fLvrdyeq"]
[FEN "7k/3p2pp/4p3/8/2Qp2P1/4b3/2P2rPP/6K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7k/3p2pp/4p3/8/2Qp2P1/4b3/2P2rPP/6K1_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ His rook sits on f2 with a discovered check loaded behind it. Before anything else: what is he actually threatening? [%csl Rf2,Re3] }
1. Kh1 { Step off the diagonal first. The discovered check is dead, and your queen is finally free to work. [%csl Gh1] } (1. Qa6 { You attack first, and he simply steps his king aside. } 1... Kg8 2. h4 Rxc2+ { A pawn gone, with check to boot. Equal. [%csl Rc2] }) (1. Qd3 { The queen walks straight into the discovered check. } 1... Rd2+ 2. Kf1 Rxd3 { A whole queen, lost to a threat you already knew was coming. [%csl Rd3] }) (1. c3 { A pawn move, and his own pawn answers right back. } 1... d5 2. Qxd4 Bxd4 { The queen is gone. [%csl Rd4] }) (1. Qxd4 { A free pawn? Look again. } 1... Bxd4 { A whole queen given away for one pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (1. Qc8+ { A check of your own, met by a discovered check right back. } 1... Rf8+ { Rook to f8 with check, and your queen is lost. [%csl Rc8] }) (1. h3 { Air for the king, but on the wrong move — the discovered check is still loaded. } 1... Rxc2+ { Discovered check, and a pawn gone with it. [%csl Rc2] }) 1... h6 { He makes some air of his own. Now your king wants g2, and his bishop wants f4. Stop that. } 2. g3 { One pawn move does both jobs: f4 is taken away from his bishop, and g2 is free for your king. [%csl Gg2,Gf4] } (2. h3 { Air for the king, but it lets the bishop reach f4. } 2... Bf4 3. Qd3 Kg8 { About equal: the bishop gets to f4 anyway, just one tempo later. [%csl Rf4] }) (2. h4 { The pawn goes one square too far. } 2... Bf4 3. g3 Bxg3 { A pawn gone. Equal: the h pawn overreached, and his bishop grabbed g3 in return. }) (2. Qc8+ { A check that achieves nothing at all. } 2... Kh7 3. g3 Rf1+ { Equal: the check gained nothing, and his rook is still active on f1. }) (2. Qa6 { The queen goes hunting for pawns instead. } 2... Kg8 3. Qc8+ Kh7 { Equal: chasing pawns cost you time, and he simply tucks his king away safely. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: prophylaxis, stop it and push"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Vp4j0xDR/q6pj3WWp"]
[FEN "3r1rk1/1ppnq1p1/2n1bp1p/p1b1p3/2N1P2N/2P1B1P1/PP2QPBP/3R1RK1 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3r1rk1/1ppnq1p1/2n1bp1p/p1b1p3/2N1P2N/2P1B1P1/PP2QPBP/3R1RK1_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ He wants to jump his knight to g6, hitting your queen and rook at once. Find one move that stops that and pushes your own plan too. }
1... Qf7 { Queen to f7: g6 is covered, and his knight on c4 comes under attack too. It cannot retreat, or a2 falls. [%cal Gf7c4,Gf7g6] } (1... Kh7 { The king steps aside, and he trades off your good bishop. } 2. Bxc5 Qxc5 3. Ne3 { His knight settles safely on e3. Equal. [%csl Ge3] }) (1... Bxc4 { You take first, and the check that follows hurts. } 2. Qxc4+ Qf7 3. Qxf7+ Rxf7 4. Rxd7 { The knight on d7 was left hanging. A whole piece is gone. [%csl Rd7] }) (1... Bxe3 { You give up the bishop, and his knight is happy to take it back. } 2. Nxe3 Qf7 3. Bf3 { His knight is fine and your bishop is gone. Slightly worse for you. }) (1... b6 { Slow, and the knight jump to g6 is still coming. } 2. Bf3 Qe8 3. b3 { Slightly worse for you: the knight jump is only delayed, not stopped. }) (1... Rfe8 { The rook moves, and the bishop gets traded off anyway. } 2. Bf3 b5 3. Bxc5 Qxc5 { Slightly worse for you: your good bishop is traded off for nothing extra. }) 2. b3 { He guards the knight with a pawn. Take it anyway. } 2... Bxc4 { Bishop takes knight. The pawn has to take back, and once it does, c4 is loose. [%cal Gb3c4] } (2... b6 { You wait, and he tidies up his pieces for free. } 3. Bc1 Ne7 4. Bh3 { Equal: waiting let him untangle everything at no cost. }) (2... Bxe3 { The wrong bishop trades first. } 3. Nxe3 Nb6 4. Bf3 { Equal: all this bishop did was hand his knight a safe square on e3. }) (2... Ba7 { The bishop steps back instead. } 3. Bxa7 Nxa7 4. Bf3 { Slightly worse for you: the bishop trade left the knight on c4 completely untouched. }) (2... b5 { The pawn hits the knight, and the bishop trades off first. } 3. Bxc5 Nxc5 4. Ne3 { Slightly worse for you: the knight found a fine new home on c5 instead. }) 3. bxc4 { He takes back with the pawn. Now the other bishop. } 3... Bxe3 { Bishop takes bishop, and his queen recaptures on e3. Then the c4 pawn falls next. [%cal Gf7c4] } (3... b6 { Slow: the pawn move does nothing while the bishop on e3 still hangs. } 4. Bc1 Ne7 5. Bh3 { Equal: the spare tempo let him reroute his knight to safety. }) (3... Ba7 { You keep the bishop, but lose the pawn on c4. } 4. Bxa7 Nxa7 5. Bf3 { Slightly worse for you: you keep the bishop pair, but the c4 pawn is gone. }) (3... Ncb8 { The knight retreats all the way home. } 4. Bf3 Bxe3 5. Bh5 { Slightly worse for you: the retreat cost a tempo he never had to spend. }) 4. Qxe3 { He recaptures with the queen. One more pawn is still there for the taking. } 4... Qxc4 { A pawn up, and his light squares are full of holes. [%csl Gc4] } (4... b6 { You wait, and he simply guards the pawn. } 5. Qe2 Nc5 6. Nf5 { The pawn is safe now. Only slightly better for you. [%csl Gc4] }) (4... Ne7 { The knight moves, and c5 comes for him. } 5. Bh3 Nb6 6. c5 { Equal: the pawn push to c5 wins back just what you took. }) (4... Nb6 { The knight hits c4, and the pawn simply runs. } 5. c5 Na4 6. Rd5 { Equal: the pawn escapes, and his rook finds an active square in the bargain. }) 0-1


